1. `Passing through referece` : we can pass can pass any variable of any data type with reference 
    in a function using `&` and we can even pass any container or even pointers in a function by 
    reference but we usually pass that variables or containers with reference only either they has
    multiple elements present in them (usually containers) or we have to modify that data type or 
    container and even reflect back (means we have to take output from the function also). Because 
    by passing with reference wherever require by condition this will save copying and make our 
    function more optimised. 
    
    
2. `Backtracking in cp`: This concept requires when there are multiple possibilites could be drawn
    after a certain step and if this happen we use backtracking . This is nothing but `pop_back()`
    then we will go through all first possibility and then popback() all first possibilities and 
    then go with second option for all things . 
    
	1. `Example` : below code is the solution for a standard cp problem for backtracking that is 
	generating parenthesis . First we will define a function in terms of its own function because 
	that is the core idea of recursion . considering n as 2 then first `"",2,2`  will be called and 
	then  `"(",1,2` then `"((",0,2` then `"(()",0,1` then `"(())",0,0` then this will get into vector
	d and then its control will goes to open and then this `"(()",0,1` because of `pop_back` and then
	`"(( ",0,2` and then its control will goes to close despite close won't run but the remaining lines
	that are `pop_back`  because recursion is now going back and this will run all not runed lines then
	`"((",2,2` this will get `"(",2,2` and `"",2,2` now very first `"(",1,2` will complete lits remaining
	lines that is `"(",1,2` will become `"()",1,1` and then `"()(",1,1` and then`()(),0,0` this will hit 
	return and this again go back and then remove all parenthesis and our string s will get empty again. 
	How emptying is string possible because we are not making a separate copy of a string for our recursion
	we are passing it with reference that is using ampersand because this will make our recursion more optimised
	as well as this will allow us backtracking. 
	
	```cpp
vector <string> d;
void generate(string &s , int open , int close){
    if(open == 0 && close == 0){
        d.push_back(s);
        return ;
    }
    if(open > 0){
        s.push_back('(');
        generate(s,open-1,close);
        s.pop_back();
    }
    if(close > 0){
        if(open < close){
            s.push_back(')');
            generate(s,open,close-1);
            s.pop_back();
        }
    }
}
void solve(){
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    string s;
    generate(s,n,n);
    for(auto &c : d){
        cout << c << '\n';
    }
    
}
	   ```
